Status: DONE (implemented, measured and deployed; human listening not claimed)
Milestone: Reduce brief-stall crackles, volume clicks and overloaded audio peaks
Dependencies: 08_POWER_LIFECYCLE, 09_NATIVE_SETTINGS, 10_DARK_ROOM
Purpose: Address reported intermittent distorted/buzzing sound without changing
  saves, game speed, CRT defaults or the user's volume/mute preferences.

Tasks:
- [x] Inspect the actual pinned runtime's config, scheduler and source gains.
- [x] Generate an original optional PSG tone without changing default ROM bytes.
- [x] Compare real protected scheduling with an unprotected pinned control.
- [x] Add bounded pre-roll and preserve valid native offsets.
- [x] Smooth overlapping volume/mute and retain them on replacement sources.
- [x] Add peak compression without trying to classify valid music as noise.
- [x] Measure native PCM/recovery and actual OfflineAudioContext output.
- [x] Preserve settings, pause, restore, power and existing full release gates.
- [x] Deploy and verify live before final public documentation.

Acceptance criteria:
- A controlled 80 ms first-scheduling stall retains a future playback deadline.
- The same unprotected pinned-runtime control misses its playback deadline.
- Actual original-tone PCM recovers after forced 250 ms starvation.
- Volume/mute transitions are continuous and do not replace the game runtime.
- Settled overloaded test output is below full scale; this is not a universal
  brick-wall limiter or a listening-quality guarantee.
- Existing saves, default demo identity, input, settings, CRT and power survive.
- Severe sustained overload/device faults and the approximately 0.1 second
  sound-latency tradeoff are documented.
- Technical evidence is distinct from reproducing the user's audible symptom.

